From a2fd4e2a8c4e9eb70ce38eda85fcfdf84407df7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 09:25:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cursor: stop stale-context resumes and split parallel tool
 batches

Two defects surfaced together in production traffic on 15.204.94.214.

1. lookupPrefixResume treated a request the checkpoint already covered end
   to end as a continuation and resumed it carrying the literal text
   "Continue.". Every repeated request - a retry, a polling monitor, two
   agents opening with the same turn - therefore replayed a finished
   conversation and answered that stale context instead of the request in
   hand ("I don't have any task to continue"). The replay also rewrote the
   prompt, so the provider-side cache missed and the turn was billed as a
   fresh cache write. Repeating one prompt five times reproduced it: only
   the first answered correctly, and the prompt grew every turn as the
   nudge and its wrong answer were appended. Boundaries that fold to an
   empty tail are now skipped, so the probe keeps walking to a shorter
   boundary that carries a real instruction, and a fully covered request
   runs fresh. The same five repeats now all answer correctly and all keep
   their ~99.5% cache read.

2. The read loop paused the segment on the first client tool call it saw.
   A parallel batch arrives as back-to-back frames that usually land in
   separate body reads, so only the batch's first call reached the client
   per round trip: asking for three get_weather calls in one reply returned
   Paris on turn 1, Tokyo on turn 2 and Lima on turn 3. Body reads move to
   a dedicated goroutine feeding a channel so the consumer can bound its
   wait, and the loop now drains for parallelToolGrace after the first tool
   call before parking. The mid-turn checkpoint, pending-usage flush and
   pause bookkeeping still run once, after the drain.
